  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1253 PM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nFriday night through Sunday...Upper ridge flattens out somewhat but \nstill remains in somewhat the same position. This will mean very hot \nconditions across the forecast area both Sat and Sun. Precipitation \nchances look meager with just isolated chances over the mts for Sat, \nthen a slight uptick in activity for Sun though it does not as \npromising as it did just 24 hrs ago. Spotty fire weather conditions \nmay develop for the far eastern plains near the KS border due to \nstrengthening southerly winds and RH levels bottoming out, so \nadditional fire weather highlights are likely from following shifts. \nPlan on highs both days in the upper 80s to mid 90s for the high \nvalleys, and 96-106F across the plains.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...Upper ridge becomes more amplified again to \nstart the work week, while the center of the high continues right \nover the UT/AZ border. Long range models still want to increase \nprecip chances over the higher terrain both days into the likely \ncategory, with some isolated activity spilling over onto the I-25 \nCorridor. The far eastern plains will likely remain dry. There will \nbe a slight increase in llvl moisture, so the hope is that RH levels \nremain above the critical sub-15% range. Temps will remain hot, \nthough there may be a few degrees of cooling in some spots, with \nhighs both days in the 80s to around 90F for the high valleys, and \n90s to low 100s for the plains.\n\nWednesday and Thursday...Upper ridge finally starts to make a slow \nmove to the east by mid-week, crossing directly over the Four \nCorners. If this model solution holds true, then afternoon high \ntemps will cool by at least 10 degrees if not more, and precip \nchances jump up into the categorical range for the higher terrain, \nwith scattered chances spreading across the plains. Area burn scars \nwill need to be monitored closely both days. Moore",
